1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Why did that moment matter?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
MatchMind makes the explanation checkable.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
This is a five-minute synthetic replay,
with fictional teams.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Play, pause, change speed, or reset.
Statistics follow the server clock.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:27,000
An insight explains a recorded window.
Its label states how it was produced.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Live model, rule fallback, and precomputed
results remain visibly distinct.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Open the evidence and select an event.
The recorded point appears on the pitch.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The clock stays where it is.
These are event locations, not tracking.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Switch from Casual to Analyst.
The wording changes for the audience.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Both versions use the same facts.
Switching makes no model request.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Evidence checked identifies the review.
Code checks references and metrics.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Live results also require agent review.
Neither proves tactical causality.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,000
This next segment is an offline demo.
There is no live model in this process.

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The result is labelled Rule fallback.
The replay continues when AI is unavailable.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
In controlled offline tests, the verifier
caught all twenty-one deliberate errors.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Four valid controls had zero false alarms.
Three recap prefixes were consistent.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Built with Microsoft Agent Framework.
Deployed on Render.

18
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
One historical sample informed comparisons.
A prototype, with published limits.
